"""Shared editorial rule: define the term, not a paper's operational usage."""
import re
TERM_MEANING_CHECK = '''NAMED CHECK: Term meaning, not study usage. FAIL if a definition narrows a general headword to one paper's usage, operationalization, example, population, apparatus or special case, or describes a study rather than the term. A sentence reporting a study's finding is not dictionary meaning and must FAIL. Source entailment alone is insufficient: the packet must explain the general headword sense. An example may illustrate an already complete general definition, but cannot replace or narrow it. Do not rescue a mismatch by prefacing it with source-relative language. A naturally specific technical headword may have a specific definition; do not confuse specificity in the headword itself with narrowing a general headword.'''
DRAFT_RULE = '''Write a standalone definition of the headword, never an account of a paper. Ban 'in this article', 'in this study', 'in this source', 'in this packet', 'here refers to', 'as used in', and equivalent source-relative framing. Do not include any sentence reporting a study's finding, association, outcome, model results or what researchers did. Do not narrow a general headword to the packet's operational usage or special case. If the packet cannot support the general term's meaning without outside knowledge, return packet_sufficient=false and an empty definition. Do not invent generality to increase the pass rate.'''
_PATTERN = re.compile(r"\b(?:in\s+(?:this|the)\s+(?:article|study|source|packet)|here\s+refers\s+to|as\s+used\s+in|in\s+the\s+source\s+packet|(?:the|this)\s+(?:study|article|paper)\s+(?:found|reported|showed|demonstrated))\b", re.I)
def source_relative_fault(definition):
    return bool(_PATTERN.search(definition))
